1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,513
Four artifacts, one feature, continuing directly from 11.2's PRD, golden set and

2
00:00:05,513 --> 00:00:05,964
rubric.

3
00:00:05,964 --> 00:00:11,191
Nothing about the scope changes here: still refunds under fifty dollars, verified

4
00:00:11,191 --> 00:00:15,644
account, capped at three a day, everything else escalated to a human.

5
00:00:15,644 --> 00:00:20,678
Watch how directly each artifact here depends on something 11.2 already built,

6
00:00:20,678 --> 00:00:24,485
because your own package should show the same dependencies.

7
00:00:24,485 --> 00:00:28,551
The cost model needs the tool-call sequence from Section seven.

8
00:00:28,551 --> 00:00:33,971
The model scorecard needs the golden set's zero-tolerance bar to even know what it's

9
00:00:33,971 --> 00:00:35,004
scoring against.

10
00:00:35,004 --> 00:00:37,327
That dependency isn't a coincidence.

11
00:00:37,327 --> 00:00:42,489
It's exactly the internal consistency 11.1 asked every capstone package to have.

12
00:00:42,489 --> 00:00:47,652
If you skipped straight to this lecture without watching 11.2 first, the numbers

13
00:00:47,652 --> 00:00:52,815
here won't fully make sense, and that's the point rather than a gap to apologise

14
00:00:52,815 --> 00:00:53,073
for.

15
00:00:53,073 --> 00:00:58,429
A real feature's cost, risk and metrics genuinely can't be judged in isolation from

16
00:00:58,429 --> 00:01:01,010
its spec and its evidence that it works.

17
00:01:01,010 --> 00:01:06,302
Treat that dependency as a feature of the format, not an inconvenience of how this

18
00:01:06,302 --> 00:01:08,754
course happened to split two lectures.

19
00:01:09,304 --> 00:01:14,341
Three tool calls, checking the account, checking the policy and cap status, issuing

20
00:01:14,341 --> 00:01:19,318
the approval, are the same three 7.6 used to explain this exact feature's latency.

21
00:01:19,318 --> 00:01:24,416
The cost model and the latency budget are built from the identical sequence, because

22
00:01:24,416 --> 00:01:28,907
they're both just different questions asked of the same three round trips.

23
00:01:28,907 --> 00:01:34,005
Thirty dollars a month sounds almost too small to justify a whole capstone artifact,

24
00:01:34,005 --> 00:01:36,857
and that's the actual point worth sitting with.

25
00:01:36,857 --> 00:01:41,834
A support agent's loaded time runs to real money per hour, and if deflection lands

26
00:01:41,834 --> 00:01:46,749
anywhere near forty five percent of two thousand monthly requests, that's roughly

27
00:01:46,749 --> 00:01:51,119
nine hundred cases a month closing without a human touching them at all.

28
00:01:51,119 --> 00:01:56,035
Thirty dollars against that much recovered time is not a close call, and it's the

29
00:01:56,035 --> 00:02:01,011
kind of number you want ready before anyone in the room asks whether this is worth

30
00:02:01,011 --> 00:02:01,558
building.

31
00:02:01,558 --> 00:02:04,592
Notice what this number can't tell you on its own.

32
00:02:04,592 --> 00:02:08,476
It's an estimate of cost, not proof the deflection rate is real.

33
00:02:08,476 --> 00:02:13,271
That gap is exactly what the metric tree later in this lecture exists to close.

34
00:02:13,821 --> 00:02:18,813
A cost model that only counts the model's own API bill is answering a narrower

35
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:23,997
question than "what does this feature cost," and saying so plainly is better than

36
00:02:23,997 --> 00:02:28,349
letting a small, honest number imply a completeness it doesn't have.

37
00:02:28,899 --> 00:02:33,977
Read the first data column before any other, because it's the only one that gets to

38
00:02:33,977 --> 00:02:35,568
disqualify a row outright.

39
00:02:35,568 --> 00:02:40,157
Budget's two wrongful approvals fail 11.2's zero-tolerance bar on the spot,

40
00:02:40,157 --> 00:02:45,296
regardless of how cheap it is or how respectable eighty eight percent sounds next to

41
00:02:45,296 --> 00:02:46,153
the other two.

42
00:02:46,153 --> 00:02:50,987
Between the two models left standing, the decision is now genuinely about cost,

43
00:02:50,987 --> 00:02:54,658
because both already cleared the bar that actually mattered.

44
00:02:54,658 --> 00:02:59,736
Flagship's extra four percentage points of overall pass rate, and its extra two and

45
00:02:59,736 --> 00:03:04,815
a half cents per resolution, are buying correctness on cases this feature's bounded

46
00:03:04,815 --> 00:03:07,262
scope was already designed to make easy.

47
00:03:07,262 --> 00:03:12,341
Mid-tier wins, not because it's cheaper in the abstract, but because the task's own

48
00:03:12,341 --> 00:03:17,358
narrowness means the cheaper model didn't have to give up anything on the row that

49
00:03:17,358 --> 00:03:18,337
actually counts.

50
00:03:18,337 --> 00:03:21,580
This is the direct payoff of 11.2's scope discipline.

51
00:03:21,580 --> 00:03:26,475
A wide-open version of this feature would need Flagship's full reasoning just to

52
00:03:26,475 --> 00:03:28,494
approach zero wrongful approvals.

53
00:03:28,494 --> 00:03:33,389
The bounded version made a cheaper model's ceiling high enough to clear the same

54
00:03:33,389 --> 00:03:33,633
bar.

55
00:03:34,183 --> 00:03:38,902
Swapping Flagship for Mid-tier barely moves the number a customer actually feels,

56
00:03:38,902 --> 00:03:43,738
which is one more reason the safety gate and the cost column are doing all the real

57
00:03:43,738 --> 00:03:45,661
work in this particular decision.

58
00:03:46,211 --> 00:03:51,447
These are 8.1's exact five categories, not a reinvented list, because the point of a

59
00:03:51,447 --> 00:03:56,620
shared register format is that any two features' risks line up in the same rows and

60
00:03:56,620 --> 00:03:58,178
can actually be compared.

61
00:03:58,178 --> 00:04:01,233
The first row is already handled, not just named.

62
00:04:01,233 --> 00:04:06,469
11.2's read-then-act separation is the mitigation, built into the design before this

63
00:04:06,469 --> 00:04:11,705
register was even written, which is what a genuinely low residual risk looks like on

64
00:04:11,705 --> 00:04:13,824
this row rather than an open item.

65
00:04:13,824 --> 00:04:18,935
The second row, data and privacy, is 8.3's territory: send the model only what the

66
00:04:18,935 --> 00:04:24,171
decision needs, verification status, today's approval count, the amount, rather than

67
00:04:24,171 --> 00:04:27,911
the full order history with stored payment details attached.

68
00:04:27,911 --> 00:04:32,773
A refund decision doesn't need to see a card number to get made correctly, and

69
00:04:32,773 --> 00:04:37,884
anything the model never receives can't leak in a response or sit exposed in a log

70
00:04:37,884 --> 00:04:38,258
later.

71
00:04:38,808 --> 00:04:43,961
The third row is easy to skip entirely on a feature this small, which is exactly why

72
00:04:43,961 --> 00:04:46,047
it belongs on the register anyway.

73
00:04:46,047 --> 00:04:50,893
Refund handling and disclosure rules aren't identical everywhere, so the honest

74
00:04:50,893 --> 00:04:55,924
mitigation is a named check before this feature ever expands past its home market,

75
00:04:55,924 --> 00:04:59,666
not an assumption that what's legal here is legal everywhere.

76
00:04:59,666 --> 00:05:04,451
The fourth row asks a question an aggregate pass rate can't answer on its own.

77
00:05:04,451 --> 00:05:09,420
A ninety six percent overall pass rate could still be hiding one customer segment

78
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:14,206
escalated far more often than another, the same blind spot 8.5 warned a single

79
00:05:14,206 --> 00:05:15,985
headline number always risks.

80
00:05:15,985 --> 00:05:21,015
The mitigation is watching the escalation rate broken out by segment, not just the

81
00:05:21,015 --> 00:05:24,328
one number this lecture has been quoting in aggregate.

82
00:05:24,328 --> 00:05:29,297
Check your own register the same way: does its headline number hide a segment you

83
00:05:29,297 --> 00:05:30,647
haven't looked at yet?

84
00:05:30,647 --> 00:05:35,493
The last row deserves the most attention of the three, because it's the one 8.6

85
00:05:35,493 --> 00:05:38,745
warned looks completely fine right up until it isn't.

86
00:05:38,745 --> 00:05:43,714
Nothing about this feature's infrastructure graphs would move if approval quality

87
00:05:43,714 --> 00:05:46,597
quietly drifted after an upstream model update.

88
00:05:46,597 --> 00:05:51,505
The mitigation has to be a metric that would actually catch it, which is exactly

89
00:05:51,505 --> 00:05:53,591
what the next slide's tree is for.

90
00:05:54,141 --> 00:05:59,419
Deflection sits at the top for the same reason 9.1 gave: it's the number that ties

91
00:05:59,419 --> 00:06:02,444
directly back to the cost model two slides ago.

92
00:06:02,444 --> 00:06:07,722
If deflection lands near the forty five percent this lecture estimated, the ninety

93
00:06:07,722 --> 00:06:12,035
hours of monthly human time this feature was built to save is real.

94
00:06:12,035 --> 00:06:17,441
If it doesn't, the cost model's promise was a hypothesis that didn't survive contact

95
00:06:17,441 --> 00:06:18,471
with production.

96
00:06:18,471 --> 00:06:23,492
Notice the second branch isn't 9.1's original acceptance metric, it's a direct

97
00:06:23,492 --> 00:06:24,264
replacement.

98
00:06:24,264 --> 00:06:29,542
What do you watch instead, when there's no human in the loop to measure acceptance

99
00:06:29,542 --> 00:06:29,864
from?

100
00:06:29,864 --> 00:06:35,207
Acceptance assumes a human is deciding, and this feature's auto-approve path has no

101
00:06:35,207 --> 00:06:40,099
such human, so the honest adaptation is the production version of 11.2's own

102
00:06:40,099 --> 00:06:44,862
zero-tolerance bar: wrongful-approval rate, watched continuously, the same

103
00:06:44,862 --> 00:06:48,531
discipline 8.6 asked of exactly this kind of quiet drift.

104
00:06:48,531 --> 00:06:53,938
The third branch keeps something acceptance-shaped for the half of this feature that

105
00:06:53,938 --> 00:06:59,216
does involve a human: when a case escalates, does the reviewer agree it needed to.

106
00:06:59,216 --> 00:07:04,172
A falling agreement rate here is an early warning that the agent's escalation

107
00:07:04,172 --> 00:07:09,579
judgement is drifting, well before it shows up as a wrongful approval anywhere else.

108
00:07:10,129 --> 00:07:14,953
Say the honest caveat, because it's the one place this whole worked package could

109
00:07:14,953 --> 00:07:18,050
quietly overstate itself if nobody said it out loud.

110
00:07:18,050 --> 00:07:22,875
Every number in the cost model two slides back was an estimate: a volume guess, a

111
00:07:22,875 --> 00:07:25,079
deflection guess, a time-saved guess.

112
00:07:25,079 --> 00:07:29,903
None of them were measured, because the feature hadn't shipped yet when they were

113
00:07:29,903 --> 00:07:30,379
written.

114
00:07:30,379 --> 00:07:32,405
That's not a flaw in the artifact.

115
00:07:32,405 --> 00:07:35,502
It's exactly what a cost model is for at this stage.

116
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:40,445
But calling it a result instead of a projection is precisely the gap 10.3 warned an

117
00:07:40,445 --> 00:07:45,032
entire roadmap against, now showing up in a single feature's numbers instead.

118
00:07:45,032 --> 00:07:49,796
The fix is the same one 10.3 gave a whole quarter: say plainly which numbers are

119
00:07:49,796 --> 00:07:54,442
measured and which are still projected, and revisit the projection against the

120
00:07:54,442 --> 00:07:59,267
metric tree's real deflection number once there's enough production data to trust

121
00:07:59,267 --> 00:07:59,445
it.

122
00:07:59,995 --> 00:08:04,547
That's the honest state worth leaving your own capstone in, projected numbers

123
00:08:04,547 --> 00:08:08,034
labelled as projections, not quietly dressed up as results.

124
00:08:08,034 --> 00:08:12,586
Most first-pass cost models, checked this way, turn out to be almost entirely

125
00:08:12,586 --> 00:08:13,236
underlined.

126
00:08:13,236 --> 00:08:15,955
That's fine at this stage of a feature's life.

127
00:08:15,955 --> 00:08:20,743
What matters is knowing which parts of your own package are still hypotheses, the

128
00:08:20,743 --> 00:08:25,708
same discipline this lecture just applied to its own numbers rather than only asking

129
00:08:25,708 --> 00:08:26,299
it of you.

130
00:08:26,849 --> 00:08:28,609
One thing before you move on.

131
00:08:28,609 --> 00:08:33,707
Look at your own model scorecard and check whether cost was compared before or after

132
00:08:33,707 --> 00:08:36,134
any safety gate you should have applied.

133
00:08:36,134 --> 00:08:40,564
If a cheaper model ever won by skipping that order, revisit the decision.

134
00:08:40,564 --> 00:08:45,601
Then label your own cost model honestly, the same exercise this lecture just ran on

135
00:08:45,601 --> 00:08:46,026
itself.

136
00:08:46,026 --> 00:08:50,881
It costs a few minutes and it's the difference between a projection and a claim.

137
00:08:50,881 --> 00:08:55,675
Next lecture is the self-assessment rubric: checking your own finished capstone

138
00:08:55,675 --> 00:08:59,377
against the same bar this worked solution just tried to meet.
